WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.960
- Some art idealizes and some
art focuses on the messiness.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.782
And I feel drawn to portray the messiness.

00:00:08.782 --> 00:00:11.558
I'm most interested in the human drama.

00:00:11.558 --> 00:00:13.930
That's always where I
wanna start is the eyes;

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.230
which is where the expression starts,

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.843
which is where the emotion is.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.970
Finding the moments where my
characters are trapped by their

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.530
own humanity is one of my favorite things.

00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:26.940
I think the overall message
is that humans are absurd,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:27.773
and it's okay.

00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:29.950
We're all in this murky soup together,

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.422
and so to have my work
impact someone’s day,

00:00:32.422 --> 00:00:35.243
I try to really remember that
that's the greatest part.

00:00:39.381 --> 00:00:40.214
My name is Kendra.

00:00:40.214 --> 00:00:41.110
I'm a cartoonist,

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.067
and I'm on the Pacific Crest Trail

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:45.119
hiking for five and a half months.

00:00:45.119 --> 00:00:47.866
My husband and I are on a journey from

00:00:47.866 --> 00:00:50.140
the border of the United States

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.382
with Mexico to the border of Canada.

00:00:51.382 --> 00:00:54.896
We've gone through desert,
and mountains, and snow,

00:00:54.896 --> 00:00:57.842
and rivers, and forest and lava rock.

00:00:57.842 --> 00:01:02.307
- If you're stuck in the
back country, in the snow,

00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:05.645
with her, she can map and
compass her way out of anywhere.

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:09.757
She's walked about 1,820 miles

00:01:09.757 --> 00:01:11.695
in the last four months or so,

00:01:11.695 --> 00:01:13.906
and pretty much dragged me along.

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:15.670
- (Kendra) Gone through
two pairs of shoes.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:16.503
On my third.

00:01:16.503 --> 00:01:19.052
- (Evan) She's incredibly
smart, and funny,

00:01:19.052 --> 00:01:21.185
and remarkably driven.

00:01:21.185 --> 00:01:23.352
- I've almost filled up two sketchbooks,

00:01:23.352 --> 00:01:24.990
which feels like a good amount of work

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.610
for the amount of time that
I've been on the trail.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:27.582
I have a third one on the way.

00:01:27.582 --> 00:01:29.660
I was hoping that walking the trail

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.530
would focus me more on the scenery,

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.580
but I still am drawn
to drawing the people.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.480
And drawing the
contradiction of, you know,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.186
you're in a beautiful
spot and it's amazing and

00:01:38.186 --> 00:01:40.598
one should be full of awe and reverence.

00:01:40.598 --> 00:01:43.530
And one is still annoyed at their husband

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.593
for not telling us where the water was.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:48.770
Every part of the trail is beautiful,

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.622
but there are some that are
more beautiful than others.

00:01:50.622 --> 00:01:52.780
Tomorrow, finally, we're gonna get

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.810
out of the trees a little bit,

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.229
and actually get to see Crater Lake.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:58.074
Crater Lake is supposed
to be just gorgeous.

00:01:58.074 --> 00:01:59.880
One of those sorta crown jewels

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.077
that you just get to walk along.

00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:02.760
You good with taking some time

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.092
when we get up there so I can draw?

00:02:04.092 --> 00:02:04.925
- Yeah.

00:02:04.925 --> 00:02:06.510
Um, get some coffee from the cafe?

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.343
- Get some coffee.

00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:08.176
Eat some snacks.

00:02:08.176 --> 00:02:09.009
- Eat some snacks.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.440
- (Kendra) Along the
trail there are the people

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.580
who are not hiking the trail,

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.598
but who are helping out hikers.

00:02:13.598 --> 00:02:15.433
We call them trail angels.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.477
- Can't believe it's beer and fruit.

00:02:18.477 --> 00:02:20.446
- And one thing that they do for

00:02:20.446 --> 00:02:22.700
hikers is provide trail magic.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.603
Any delightful thing that
sorta helps the hikers along.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:27.949
- Cheers my lady.

00:02:27.949 --> 00:02:28.782
- Cheers!

00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:29.615
- To us.

00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:30.500
- All the way to California.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.718
Man, we're thankful for them.

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:34.793
No hiker I know has ever
turned down a cold beer.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.950
We come across this amazing
trail magic, and I'm like,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.833
ah, unlabeled weird thing you add water to.

00:02:39.833 --> 00:02:42.000
Let's find out what it is.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.290
Drawing a cartoon about a life experience,

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.469
which is what I'm doing on the trail,

00:02:46.469 --> 00:02:49.683
you are the architect of the
experience and the memory.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.750
I mean a trip like this is
such a extended metaphor,

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.830
which escapes no one, right?

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:56.881
You're going on the physical journey,

00:02:56.881 --> 00:02:58.954
and you're also going
on the internal journey.

00:02:58.954 --> 00:03:00.124
I think we're almost there.

00:03:00.124 --> 00:03:03.791
Da, da, da, da, da, da, da!

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.373
Nice!

00:03:09.373 --> 00:03:10.560
Nice!

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.003
Can we jump in it?

00:03:12.003 --> 00:03:13.741
- Yeah, there's a trail down.

00:03:13.741 --> 00:03:15.023
- Oh, nice.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:19.410
To get to really interesting ideas

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.280
you have to let time not dictate you.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.133
You have to have a
certain amount of freedom.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:36.500
It's beautiful, and it's very simple.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.800
Your only goal is to
move forward everyday.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.419
I've just gotten to
the point where drawing

00:03:41.419 --> 00:03:43.780
is part of the way I
interact with the world,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.218
and how I think through
my own experiences.

00:03:45.218 --> 00:03:47.438
Being awake and alive everyday makes

00:03:47.438 --> 00:03:49.513
me wanna interact with
a piece of paper also.

00:03:49.513 --> 00:03:52.190
I mean I’ve just put myself in the most

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:53.142
beautiful place I can imagine.

00:03:53.142 --> 00:03:56.363
That's kind of the goal is to have
as much of this as possible.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.700
This is the great shakeup that then I

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.223
get to digest for a long time.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:03.074
I think after I'm off the trail

00:04:03.074 --> 00:04:04.640
I'll look through
everything that I've done,

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.419
and sort of decide where I want it to live.

00:04:06.419 --> 00:04:08.330
So that it can have a life of

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.030
its own outside of my sketchbook.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.665
I'm looking forward to
getting back into my routine.

00:04:12.665 --> 00:04:16.283
Do that more practiced discovery.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.120
I bet after this trip I'll be satiated

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.176
for at least a couple months.

00:04:20.176 --> 00:04:22.190
I bet it'll be a little while before

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.707
I start pestering Evan
about our next adventure.

